# JUranometria's Norwegian Bokmal interface (Sprint 33, issue #350).
#
# Registering this language is what makes it selectable. Nothing
# lists the interface languages: the build walks this directory and
# writes the index runtime reads, so a language arrives by being
# installed - the same contract the chart language packs keep.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# status=reviewed, and what that means
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The strings in nb-NO.properties are ORIGINAL TRANSLATIONS written
# for this application. They are not taken from a source, so unlike
# the constellation names next door they carry no citation and cannot
# be corroborated against one: their accuracy is a matter of judgement
# about Norwegian, and that judgement is the owner's.
#
# The owner, who reads Norwegian, has now been through every surface
# this sprint covers and said these are the words an atlas should
# use. That is what "reviewed" records - and it is deliberately not
# "verified", which in this repository means checked against a source
# that could contradict it. There is no such source here. Calling
# linguistic judgement "verified" would borrow a word that promises
# corroboration nobody can perform.
#
# This file is the single place that status lives. The twelve study
# companions read it through juranometria.tool.InterfaceLanguageStatus
# and none of them states a status of its own, so a companion cannot
# say one thing while the descriptor says another. An unrecognised
# status refuses rather than printing itself.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# What is covered
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Every reader-facing surface the application owns: the menu bar,
# Settings, Chart Options, the Chart Keyboard, the toolbar and its
# search field, the Inspector, On This Page, Place and Time, About
# (summary and notices), exporting a sheet, the drawn and exported
# page's own words, the words Swing puts around the atlas's dialogs,
# and the startup failure reporter.
#
# A key with no Norwegian value falls back to English, visibly and by
# the gate's rule, and no key ever reaches a reader as itself. That
# rule has not changed; what has changed is that no reader-facing
# application prose is relying on it any more.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately excluded, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# canonical notation and identity
#     ICRS J2000, V, B, M 31, TYC 2801-2090-1, Cl+N, SVG, PDF, A4,
#     US Letter. The same to every reader in every language. These
#     are handed to sentences as arguments and written into no
#     translated value.
#
# technical evidence
#     The studies under docs/studies, this repository's decision
#     records, and the developer entry points that generate them.
#     They address a maintainer, not a reader.
#
# the operating system's own menu
#     On macOS the application menu's first items - About, Services,
#     Hide, Quit - are drawn by the platform from its own resources
#     in the desktop's language. The atlas does not own them and
#     cannot translate them without lying about what it controls.
#
# the emergency English fallback
#     StartupFailure carries one embedded English headline, reached
#     when opening a file, a preference node or a language class is
#     what failed. It is translated as startup.failed and used when
#     the machinery works; the embedded copy is the half that must
#     survive the machinery not working.
#
# four surfaces deferred by review
#     The eighteen access letters and Swing's mnemonic keys; the
#     Chart Keyboard's stable-help and transient-status split; Place
#     and Time's silent restoration of invalid input; and the
#     constellation search of #356. Each is a behaviour change as
#     much as a translation, and each is recorded in its own issue.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Not a chart language
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This governs the words on the controls. What the sky is called is a
# separate setting a reader chooses separately, and lives under
# resources/sky-language. Registering Norwegian here does, however,
# make "Follow interface" mean something it could not mean before:
# a reader whose chart language follows their interface now gets
# Norwegian constellation names by choosing a Norwegian interface.

interface-schema=1
tag=nb-NO
display-name=Norsk bokmål
strings=nb-NO.properties
status=reviewed
